From: Husky <hyiy666@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_qdisc: use usleep_range_idle() for stats sync pacing

nss_qdisc_stats_sync_process_work() runs in a workqueue and paces the
NSS stats sync requests with usleep_range(4900, 5000). usleep_range()
sleeps in TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE, and while qdisc statistics sync is
active the work runs at a high rate, so the 5s loadavg sampler
frequently catches the kworker in D-state, inflating the load average
despite mostly idle CPUs.

usleep_range_idle() (available since kernel 5.16) sleeps in TASK_IDLE
(TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_NOLOAD): identical semantics for a
kworker (which never receives signals), but excluded from load average
accounting.

This mirrors the same change already applied to the qca-nss-ecm stats
sync workers.

Signed-off-by: Husky <hyiy666@gmail.com>
--- a/nss_qdisc/nss_qdisc_stats.c
+++ b/nss_qdisc/nss_qdisc_stats.c
@@ -534,7 +534,7 @@ static void nss_qdisc_stats_sync_process_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	 * to space the sync command requests to NSS and also ease the workload
 	 * on the CPU
 	 */
-	usleep_range(NSS_QDISC_STATS_SYNC_MANY_UDELAY - 100, NSS_QDISC_STATS_SYNC_MANY_UDELAY);
+	usleep_range_idle(NSS_QDISC_STATS_SYNC_MANY_UDELAY - 100, NSS_QDISC_STATS_SYNC_MANY_UDELAY);
 
 	spin_lock_bh(&nss_qdisc_stats_list_lock);
 	if (list_empty(&nss_qdisc_stats_list)) {
